Transitive Euler Fee Flow provenance and licensing blocker
----------------------------------------------------------

give.fun Auction.sol states that it is forked and modified from Euler Fee Flow. Liquid Signal Governance Strategy.sol
likewise identifies Euler Finance's FeeFlow as its ancestor. The matching transitive source reviewed for provenance is:

- Repository: https://github.com/euler-xyz/fee-flow
- Commit: 3bee858a1568d1313f37d615953f83391a897866
- src/FeeFlowController.sol
  SHA-256: b2ae9c1067bd6b6964bb1e91776d181ac89eefca69c9e3795f9229b393067abc
- File SPDX: GPL-2.0-or-later
- Repository license at that pin: GNU GPL version 2, with the file's "or later" designation

The pinned Liquid Signal Governance files disclose two additional ancestors without enough information to pin them:

- contracts/Voter.sol says it is forked and modified from Andre Cronje's Solidly.
- contracts/Bribe.sol says it is forked and modified from Synthetix StakingRewards.

Liquid Signal Governance does not identify an exact ancestor repository, commit, path, or separate permission for
either statement. This notice deliberately does not invent those pins or infer which historical version was used.
Identifying the exact ancestors, licenses, and required attribution remains part of the unresolved provenance review.

The reviewed give.fun and Liquid Signal Governance Solidity files carry MIT SPDX headers, but neither pinned
repository contains a tracked root LICENSE, COPYING, or NOTICE file despite broader README or repository-license
descriptions. No separate permission or dual-license evidence for the Euler-derived auction behavior has been
identified. Similar constants, ordering, and arithmetic make the transitive chain of title a material unresolved
